A||N||5||Ben84||4.9 and 3800 Heaven........||||6||
Z||000000||Ben84||03-21-2001||12:04 PM||||I had the opportunity to look for engines for a future engine swap at my local automotive salvage yards... I found one 95 Seville Northstar for 968.00 (25K miles), 4 4.9 Cadillac (low mileage), and numerous 3800 and 1 3800 Series II for (750.00), BUT there is no engine harness or ECM.  During the engine removal they cut the harness.  What is the price range of a harness/ECM for the various engines.... I'm not able to find a wreck vehicle with a quality engine so far in my treasure hunt... Thanks For Any Info..<P>[This message has been edited by Ben84 (edited 03-21-2001).]<p>[This message has been edited by Ben84 (edited 03-21-2001).]||||reg||6
Z||000001||1FST2M6||03-21-2001||01:46 PM||||a new wiring harness fromt he dealer is about $450... and you have to find an ECU.. or just buy a Haltech E6k and build your own harness... it's not as difficult as you'd think||||reg||1
Z||000002||rockcrawl||03-21-2001||04:04 PM||||Haltech, Accel, Holley, Fel-pro and many others are building fuel management systems that are fully programmable and tunable to work with just about any engine. The aftermarket harnesses will be much easier to work with than the stock ones. The price is the killer though, most are $1000 on up, which is pretty rough considering the OEM harness and ECM are usally freebies when you buy the donor engine. I've been considering the Accel DFI gen. 7.0 or Holley's Commander 950 for my 4.9 even though I have the stock ECM and harness already. Unless someone can hack the 4.9 code, I probably don't have any other choice but to use an aftermarket controller.<P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'87 GT<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/rockcrawlsig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||rockcrawl||03-21-2001||04:06 PM|||| <A HREF="http://mrgasket.com/dfinew.htm#DFI" TARGET=_blank>http://mrgasket.com/dfinew.htm#DFI</A> <BR> <A HREF="http://www.holley.com/HiOctn/ProdLine/Products/C950/C950.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.holley.com/HiOctn/ProdLine/Products/C950/C950.html</A> <BR> <A HREF="http://www.tciauto.com/ecu/index_spe.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.tciauto.com/ecu/index_spe.htm</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||pherder||03-21-2001||05:25 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by rockcrawl:<BR><B>Haltech, Accel, Holley, Fel-pro and many others are building fuel management systems that are fully programmable and tunable to work with just about any engine.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I have a Haltech E6GM ECU and found that the Fiero's V6 wiring harness is almost pin to pin compatible!!!  Only a couple of wires are different!!  For a V8 you would have to add a couple of injector plugs but all the rest of the connections are there!<P>One thing, the Haltech E6GM computer does <B>NOT</B> use an O2 sensor.  You have to program EVERYTHING to make it run correctly.<P>It will run a Bar 2 and Bar 3 MAP so supercharging/turbo charging is in the program!<P>Cost from Force Fuel Injection $695 + S/H<P>Paul<BR>------------------<BR>88 Formula<BR>88 Coupe<BR>86 SE<BR>95 GMC<BR>92 Mazda<BR>55 Jones<BR>Sedgwick, KS 67135<BR>pherder@kscable.com<p>[This message has been edited by pherder (edited 03-21-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||rockcrawl||03-21-2001||06:33 PM||||Good point Paul, I wouldn't buy a controller that doesn't use an O2 sensor. I'm leaning toward the Holley 950 right now, $520 or $900 with harness. The price and features are unbeatable. You can download free base maps for a bunch of different engines from their website, and then fine tune it from there.||||reg||1
